{#
  Global kolla-build template override for the EOL image builds.

  It is passed to every build as:

      --template-override .github/kolla/template-override.j2

  Blocks that a given image does not define are simply ignored by Jinja, so a
  single file can carry overrides for several templates.
#}
{% extends parent_template %}

{#
  1. OCI metadata on every image.

  GHCR uses org.opencontainers.image.source to link a package back to the
  repository it was built from. This replaces the stock labels block that every
  kolla Dockerfile.j2 defines.
#}
{% block labels %}
LABEL maintainer="{{ maintainer }}" \
      name="{{ image_name }}" \
      build-date="{{ build_date }}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.source="https://github.com/preinpost/kolla" \
      org.opencontainers.image.title="kolla/{{ image_name }}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.version="{{ tag }}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.revision="{{ kolla_version }}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.licenses="Apache-2.0"
{% endblock %}

{#
  2. Pin pip inside the OpenStack virtualenv (openstack-base only).

  pip >= 25.1 builds sdists that only ship a setup.py in a PEP 517 isolated
  environment. That environment resolves to today's setuptools, which no longer
  provides pkg_resources, and every XStatic-* package (needed by horizon) starts
  failing with:

      ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pkg_resources'

  pip 24.0 still uses the legacy setup.py path, which builds those sdists with
  the setuptools==67.2.* that openstack-base already pins, so the images build
  the same way they did when the release was current. Downgrading pip after the
  upstream install keeps the rest of the template untouched.
#}
{% block openstack_base_footer %}
RUN python3 -m pip --no-cache-dir install 'pip==24.0'
{% endblock %}
